@startuml
' Evidence: Laplace-MCP/laplace_mcp.py SHA256 24cfe3607f90cebff592f85f7c813a8f48ff10cfb0c70c9bcf329ee3ad0489f0
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFCFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName SansSerif
title Modular MCP — independent Python stdio admit/fetch surface
start
:Load configured liblpm; create process-local index;
if (Index unavailable?) then (yes)
 :Print tier-zero error; exit one;
 stop
endif
while (Read Content-Length framed message?) is (yes)
 :Parse JSON;
 if (Notification?) then (yes)
  :Ignore;
 elseif (initialize or tools/list?) then (yes)
  :Return protocol/server metadata or exactly admit and fetch schemas;
 elseif (tools/call?) then (yes)
  if (admit?) then (yes)
   :Index filesystem path; get last hit; fetch record summary;
   :Return ID, tier, child count, coordinate, Hilbert and path;
  elseif (fetch?) then (yes)
   :Decode exact 16-byte ID; fetch record and up to eight child IDs;
   :Return total child count plus shown child IDs;
  else (unknown)
   :Raise tool error;
  endif
  :Return content text with isError false, or catch Exception as isError true;
 else (unknown method)
  :Return method-not-found only when request ID supplied;
 endif
 :Write Content-Length response and flush;
endwhile (EOF)
stop
footer
Source-reviewed orchestration. Nested semantics remain open; no runtime acceptance
claimed. Entire Python adapter reviewed. It exposes two tools and a process-local liblpm
index, separate from the native ten-tool HTTP server. Admit does not check its final
lpm_fetch return code. Framing/JSON errors outside the tool-call try can terminate
serving; liblpm storage and recipe internals remain nested.
end footer

@enduml
